WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.901 --> 00:00:10.523
I come from Bali, a very small 
island in Indonesia.

00:00:10.523 --> 00:00:13.130
The island is mostly known 
around the world

00:00:13.130 --> 00:00:16.500
as a very popular tourist destination,

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:19.600
with its beautiful landscapes, 
clear blue beaches,

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:25.372
and most importantly, 
its very unique culture and tradition.

00:00:25.372 --> 00:00:28.782
Every single year in Bali,
we celebrate the Balinese New Year,

00:00:28.782 --> 00:00:31.983
also known as Nyepi.

00:00:31.983 --> 00:00:35.892
It is a day of silence 
for the Balinese people;

00:00:35.892 --> 00:00:38.172
no one is allowed to leave their houses,

00:00:38.172 --> 00:00:40.511
no one is allowed to light any fire,

00:00:40.511 --> 00:00:43.504
or use any devices 
that are emitting light,

00:00:43.504 --> 00:00:46.591
including television and lamps.

00:00:46.591 --> 00:00:50.513
During the day it would be very quiet 
and peaceful;

00:00:50.513 --> 00:00:54.782
there'd be no more sounds of cars 
or motorbikes passing by.

00:00:54.782 --> 00:01:01.933
At night it would be pitch black, 
complete darkness.

00:01:01.933 --> 00:01:04.043
There would be no form of lights,

00:01:04.043 --> 00:01:08.295
not even the light of the moon 
since it’s also during the lunar eclipse.

00:01:08.295 --> 00:01:13.733
Now, as a very young child, 
I’ve always been terrified of the dark,

00:01:13.733 --> 00:01:15.973
and I couldn’t bear the thought

00:01:15.973 --> 00:01:22.144
that I had to go through these same nights
every single year.

00:01:22.144 --> 00:01:25.064
I’ve always thought that we were alone,

00:01:25.064 --> 00:01:29.703
that we were the only people in the world
still living like cavemen.

00:01:29.703 --> 00:01:31.085
But the older I got,

00:01:31.085 --> 00:01:37.584
the more I realized that one day in a year
without light is not so bad after all.

00:01:37.584 --> 00:01:41.455
I realized that I was in fact not alone,

00:01:41.455 --> 00:01:45.915
and that there are over a billion people 
around the world

00:01:45.915 --> 00:01:49.196
who are going through 
these same nights that I feared

00:01:49.196 --> 00:01:53.294
every single day of their lives.

00:01:53.294 --> 00:01:58.816
I’ve grown to appreciate this tradition
and see it not only as a simple holiday,

00:01:58.816 --> 00:02:04.626
but as a reminder to me that there are
people out there living without light

00:02:04.626 --> 00:02:08.364
and that we should 
do our best to help them.

00:02:08.364 --> 00:02:10.686
I’ve experienced a day 
of what it felt like

00:02:10.686 --> 00:02:14.756
for those 1.2 billion people 
around the world

00:02:14.756 --> 00:02:17.846
who have no access to electricity,

00:02:17.846 --> 00:02:19.406
And what struck me the most

00:02:19.406 --> 00:02:23.244
was finding out that a large proportion 
of these people live near the equator,

00:02:23.244 --> 00:02:27.387
where sunlight is most abundant.

00:02:27.387 --> 00:02:32.198
Coming from a tropical island myself, 
the sun has always inspired me.

00:02:32.198 --> 00:02:35.119
The amount of heat coming 
to my room in the afternoon

00:02:35.119 --> 00:02:39.364
is enough to convince me 
that the sun is indeed very powerful,

00:02:39.364 --> 00:02:40.717
and it made me wonder

00:02:40.717 --> 00:02:46.156
why is it that our use of solar energy 
today is still very minimal?

00:02:46.156 --> 00:02:49.447
To understand how we got here 
in the first place,

00:02:49.447 --> 00:02:51.476
let’s take a look at a brief history

00:02:51.476 --> 00:02:54.668
of how we humans discovered 
new sources of energy.

00:02:54.668 --> 00:02:57.175
Far before the industrial revolution,

00:02:57.175 --> 00:03:01.856
we all started off simply by burning wood 
to keep ourselves warm.

00:03:01.856 --> 00:03:03.778
In the mid-19th century,

00:03:03.778 --> 00:03:06.618
we started hunting for whales in the vast 
and empty oceans

00:03:06.618 --> 00:03:10.378
for their oils to light illuminant
lamps at home.

00:03:10.378 --> 00:03:12.559
We then explored underground 
for coal and oil

00:03:12.559 --> 00:03:14.997
to power our steam engines and factories.

00:03:14.997 --> 00:03:17.668
Recently we started harnessing 
nuclear energy

00:03:17.668 --> 00:03:19.958
to meet our growing energy consumption.

00:03:19.958 --> 00:03:23.818
What’s fascinating is that throughout 
these centuries of discoveries,

00:03:23.818 --> 00:03:28.209
the sun has always been present.

00:03:28.209 --> 00:03:31.190
it has always been right up there,

00:03:31.190 --> 00:03:37.109
but we’d rather look underground 
and underwater for energy instead.

00:03:37.109 --> 00:03:39.400
Well I’m not blaming 
the people of the past

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:41.609
because they simply 
didn’t have the technology

00:03:41.609 --> 00:03:44.300
or understanding on 
how to capture the sun’s energy.

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:49.189
But today, in a world far more 
technologically advanced than before,

00:03:49.189 --> 00:03:53.540
I believe we can do much better.

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:57.831
The economies of the world today 
are growing at an exponential rate,

00:03:57.831 --> 00:04:00.240
but the nature of that growth itself

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:07.339
is quite unhealthy and unsustainable 
due to the amount of pollution we emit.

00:04:07.339 --> 00:04:09.861
If we are ever to maintain 
this rate of growth,

00:04:09.861 --> 00:04:12.961
we need to make a change,

00:04:12.961 --> 00:04:17.071
a change towards a cleaner 
alternative energy source.

00:04:17.071 --> 00:04:18.731
Many countries around the world

00:04:18.731 --> 00:04:21.531
are already putting their best efforts 
in this transition,

00:04:21.531 --> 00:04:24.481
both for the international commitment
to reduce air pollution,

00:04:24.481 --> 00:04:28.802
and the positive prospects of solar energy
in their own economies.

00:04:28.802 --> 00:04:34.031
Portugal in May 2016 was powered 
for four days entirely

00:04:34.031 --> 00:04:38.473
from renewable energies that include wind,
hydro, and solar power.

00:04:38.473 --> 00:04:40.560
China and India are both planning

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:44.852
to nearly triple their solar capacities 
in a few years’ time.

00:04:44.852 --> 00:04:47.573
Japan started building 
floating solar farms

00:04:47.573 --> 00:04:53.553
that plans to power nearly 
5000 households by early 2018.

00:04:53.553 --> 00:04:57.149
In Germany, 7% of 
the total electricity consumption

00:04:57.149 --> 00:04:59.863
has already come from solar energy.

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:04.141
You see, these are signs.

00:05:04.141 --> 00:05:06.213
These commitments made by these countries

00:05:06.213 --> 00:05:09.784
are signs that they have realized
the importance and significance

00:05:09.784 --> 00:05:12.265
of solar energy,

00:05:12.265 --> 00:05:17.773
and now it’s up to us to decide whether 
or not we should welcome this change

00:05:17.773 --> 00:05:21.104
and whether or not we should 
push it to the next level.

00:05:21.104 --> 00:05:23.786
Many people would assume that 
a solar dependent future

00:05:23.786 --> 00:05:26.388
is too optimistic or is impossible.

00:05:26.388 --> 00:05:30.615
Well, I want you all to take 
a look at this picture right here.

00:05:30.615 --> 00:05:33.734
What do you see?

00:05:33.734 --> 00:05:37.727
Many people would say, 
"Well, that’s just a rainforest."

00:05:37.727 --> 00:05:42.286
What I see is a civilization,

00:05:42.286 --> 00:05:46.708
a very prosperous civilization which has 
flourished throughout our planet

00:05:46.708 --> 00:05:49.006
for a great deal 
of our planet’s history

00:05:49.006 --> 00:05:52.616
using the energy from the sun.

00:05:52.616 --> 00:05:55.466
And if they are capable 
of doing such things,

00:05:55.466 --> 00:05:59.402
then I believe we could do it too.

00:05:59.402 --> 00:06:05.185
In fact, the sun delivers more energy 
to our planet in just one hour,

00:06:05.185 --> 00:06:06.789
one hour,

00:06:06.789 --> 00:06:10.747
than we consume in an entire year.

00:06:10.747 --> 00:06:16.294
With our current solar technology,
which has an efficiency of around 20%,

00:06:16.294 --> 00:06:21.241
it is estimated that we need an area 
around about the size of Spain

00:06:21.241 --> 00:06:24.937
to power the entire world.

00:06:24.937 --> 00:06:28.777
Now, keep in mind that I said
with our current solar technology.

00:06:28.777 --> 00:06:35.228
If that efficiency doubles, 
then we willl need half of that area.

00:06:35.228 --> 00:06:37.535
Now, the area of Spain 
itself may look very big,

00:06:37.535 --> 00:06:40.375
but actually, if all the countries 
of the world today

00:06:40.375 --> 00:06:45.077
devote just 0.4% of their land area 
for a solar farm,

00:06:45.077 --> 00:06:49.678
that would add up to the size of Spain.

00:06:49.678 --> 00:06:52.788
And also keep in mind 
that I said land area,

00:06:52.788 --> 00:06:55.369
I didn’t even consider 
the area of the ocean,

00:06:55.369 --> 00:07:00.807
which by the way,
covers 71% of this planet’s surface.

00:07:00.807 --> 00:07:07.239
In fact, we could fit in over 700 Spains 
on our ocean today,

00:07:07.239 --> 00:07:13.200
and we only need the area of 
one Spain to power the entire world.

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:15.659
And when I talk about 
these facts about areas,

00:07:15.659 --> 00:07:19.580
I’m not only talking about those solar
farms with neatly arranged solar panels.

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:24.410
I’m also talking about the actions 
of individuals, like you and I,

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:28.782
and how significant an impact we
could make if we all acted collectively.

00:07:28.782 --> 00:07:32.612
Take a smartphone as an example.

00:07:32.612 --> 00:07:35.721
The average energy needed to charge 
a smartphone in a year

00:07:35.721 --> 00:07:38.383
is approximately three kilowatt hours.

00:07:38.383 --> 00:07:43.651
That is roughly the same energy 
that could lift a 1000 ton object,

00:07:43.651 --> 00:07:49.824
or around 180 adult elephants
one meter above the ground.

00:07:49.824 --> 00:07:55.233
Could you believe that the small phone 
you are carrying in your pockets every day

00:07:55.233 --> 00:07:57.293
takes up the same energy in a year

00:07:57.293 --> 00:08:03.463
that could lift 180 adult elephants 
one meter, or around three feet,

00:08:03.463 --> 00:08:05.914
above the ground?

00:08:05.914 --> 00:08:08.393
Now that is just one phone.

00:08:08.393 --> 00:08:12.974
There is an estimated 2.3 billion 
smartphone users in the world today,

00:08:12.974 --> 00:08:15.163
and in that case, the total energy needed

00:08:15.163 --> 00:08:17.795
to charge all the smartphones 
in the world per year

00:08:17.795 --> 00:08:21.464
would be 7 billion kilowatt hours,

00:08:21.464 --> 00:08:23.844
the same energy that could power

00:08:23.844 --> 00:08:28.794
an additional 600,000 average 
American households.

00:08:28.794 --> 00:08:32.845
We could light the homes 
of over 600,000 more people

00:08:32.845 --> 00:08:37.529
with the energy we use 
to charge our smartphones.

00:08:37.529 --> 00:08:39.686
If we were instead to charge our phones

00:08:39.686 --> 00:08:42.004
with devices like solar-powered 
battery packs,

00:08:42.004 --> 00:08:44.348
that amount of energy 
could actually be saved

00:08:44.348 --> 00:08:46.688
to help those who 
can’t afford electricity.

00:08:46.688 --> 00:08:50.363
If individuals start contributing 
in generating just small amounts of energy

00:08:50.363 --> 00:08:54.535
to power even the smallest things,
like phones and lamps,

00:08:54.535 --> 00:09:00.178
that energy saved would 
add up to something much, much bigger.

00:09:00.178 --> 00:09:02.133
Fortunately, in today’s modern world,

00:09:02.133 --> 00:09:05.586
there are already many inventions 
out there that could help us do this.

00:09:05.586 --> 00:09:08.437
An example would be 
Tesla’s solar roof tiles,

00:09:08.437 --> 00:09:10.974
which may cost more than 
regular roof tiles at first,

00:09:10.974 --> 00:09:13.157
but can actually 
be cheaper in the long run

00:09:13.157 --> 00:09:16.408
if you consider the cuts 
in your electricity bills.

00:09:16.408 --> 00:09:20.174
And with the rise of other inventions,
like solar-powered vehicles,

00:09:20.174 --> 00:09:24.463
I believe this industry has the potential
to continue growing

00:09:24.463 --> 00:09:28.280
and possibly dominate future markets.

00:09:28.280 --> 00:09:32.417
Now, let’s not only talk 
about the good things.

00:09:32.417 --> 00:09:36.287
There are many problems and limitations
to solar energy that we can’t ignore,

00:09:36.287 --> 00:09:37.820
but let’s also take a look

00:09:37.820 --> 00:09:40.740
at some possible solutions 
to overcome them, as well.

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:43.467
The first and most common argument 
against solar panels

00:09:43.467 --> 00:09:45.631
is that they are too expensive.

00:09:45.631 --> 00:09:49.023
Now, there are no disagreements that 
I can make here because they are;

00:09:49.023 --> 00:09:50.741
they are very expensive.

00:09:50.741 --> 00:09:53.560
But I can say the price of solar panels

00:09:53.560 --> 00:09:57.707
has been falling drastically 
over the past 40 years.

00:09:57.707 --> 00:10:00.927
The price of a solar panel 
back in the mid-1970s

00:10:00.927 --> 00:10:03.532
was around about 80 dollars per watt,

00:10:03.532 --> 00:10:08.168
and today that has fallen to less
than a dollar per watt.

00:10:08.168 --> 00:10:14.719
That is over 100 times cheaper 
than what it was 40 years ago.

00:10:14.719 --> 00:10:16.409
And in some countries today,

00:10:16.409 --> 00:10:19.896
solar energy is already cheaper 
than alternatives, like fossil fuel.

00:10:19.896 --> 00:10:23.621
People are no longer using solar energy 
because they are cleaner,

00:10:23.621 --> 00:10:25.565
but because they are more profitable,

00:10:27.071 --> 00:10:30.025
and let’s face it, that’s what most 
of us think about.

00:10:30.025 --> 00:10:33.295
The second problem with solar energy 
is that they are inconsistent.

00:10:33.295 --> 00:10:35.604
Solar panels don’t work as well 
on cloudy days

00:10:35.604 --> 00:10:37.484
and they don’t work at all at night,

00:10:37.484 --> 00:10:39.880
the times when most individuals 
need energy.

00:10:39.880 --> 00:10:42.026
However, with the availability 
of batteries,

00:10:42.026 --> 00:10:44.494
we can store this energy generated 
from sunny days

00:10:44.494 --> 00:10:48.226
and use them at times when we need them.

00:10:48.226 --> 00:10:51.228
And finally, the last problem associated 
with solar energy

00:10:51.228 --> 00:10:55.835
is that sunlight is unevenly distributed 
across our planet.

00:10:55.835 --> 00:10:58.262
Countries in the northern 
and southern hemispheres

00:10:58.262 --> 00:11:00.936
tend to receive less sunlight 
than those in the equator,

00:11:00.936 --> 00:11:04.696
and some of those countries may not 
be able to rely on solar energy just yet.

00:11:04.696 --> 00:11:08.277
Those countries could instead look towards
other forms of renewable energy,

00:11:08.277 --> 00:11:10.488
such as bio-fuel, geothermal or wind,

00:11:10.488 --> 00:11:14.227
until a possible solution in the future
could be found.

00:11:14.227 --> 00:11:19.387
Now, despite all these issues 
that I’ve talked about,

00:11:19.387 --> 00:11:24.965
we must all be reminded 
of a very important fact:

00:11:24.965 --> 00:11:30.699
that the world today is slowly 
running out of fossil fuel.

00:11:30.699 --> 00:11:33.427
Putting the problem of pollution aside,

00:11:33.427 --> 00:11:39.429
we must all understand that fossil fuel
cannot keep us running forever.

00:11:39.429 --> 00:11:42.419
The fossil fuel that we are using today

00:11:42.419 --> 00:11:49.100
have accumulated from the past
millions and millions of centuries,

00:11:49.100 --> 00:11:56.150
and we have used up so much 
of them in only 250 years.

00:11:56.150 --> 00:11:59.727
It is even predicted that we will 
completely run out of fossil fuel

00:11:59.727 --> 00:12:03.737
by the next 50 to 100 years,

00:12:03.737 --> 00:12:08.818
and if we are not seriously committed 
on replacing these fossil fuels,

00:12:09.370 --> 00:12:11.368
then we and the future generations

00:12:11.368 --> 00:12:16.037
may not be able to enjoy the things 
that are available to us today.

00:12:16.037 --> 00:12:20.555
Especially in a world faced with issues
like global warming and climate change,

00:12:20.555 --> 00:12:25.748
solar energy should be met 
with more open arms.

00:12:25.748 --> 00:12:32.186
And maybe one day, when the whole 
world looks up into the sun,

00:12:32.186 --> 00:12:35.758
and when those billions 
could finally light up their homes,

00:12:35.758 --> 00:12:40.920
I can once again look up into 
the night sky of Nyepi Day back in Bali,

00:12:40.920 --> 00:12:48.800
not scared, but deeply, deeply, satisfied,
knowing that we are, in fact,

00:12:48.800 --> 00:12:54.719
the only people in the world 
still living in the dark, like cavemen.

00:12:54.719 --> 00:12:55.969
Thank you.

